Spec: Spec clarifies that higher-privilege CBIE/CBCFE/CBZE fields in *envcfg CSRs must cascade-down to override lower-privilege CBO behavior; implementations checking only local *envcfg enable an untrusted lower privilege level to perform destructive cache invalidation (discarding dirty data) that the OS/hypervisor explicitly restricted.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:543-576  kvm_riscv_vcpu_setup_config
      Sets cfg->henvcfg with ENVCFG_CBIE (INV mode, value 0x3), ENVCFG_CBCFE, and ENVCFG_CBZE based solely on ISA extension availability (riscv_isa_extension_available(isa, ZICBOM) / ZICBOZ). Per RISC-V privileged spec, menvcfg.CBIE/CBCFE/CBZE fields cascade down to henvcfg — a write to henvcfg is hardware-clamped by menvcfg. If M-mode firmware has configured menvcfg to restrict CBIE to FLUSH-only (0x1) or ILL (0x0), KVM's write of INV (0x3) either gets silently clamped to FLUSH (causing the guest to believe it is invalidating cache lines when it is only flushing — silent data corruption) or ignored entirely (causing guest illegal-instruction traps — DoS). There is zero cross-check against CSR_MENVCFG before composing the henvcfg value. Only ISA bits are consulted; the cascading privilege constraint is completely ignored.
